The Nautilus DevOps team has already deployed a ReplicaSet to host an application that requires a highly available infrastructure. 
Your task is to expose the application running in the existing ReplicaSet by creating a Kubernetes NodePort Service.

Follow the specifications below to create the Service and ensure the application pods are accessible:


A ReplicaSet named nginx-replicaset is already running in the cluster.

The pods managed by the ReplicaSet use the following labels:
Assign labels app as nginx_app, and type as front-end.

Create a NodePort Service named nginx-service to expose the application.

Set the NodePort to 30080.

Expose port 80 of the application.

Note: Do not delete or modify the configuration of the deployed ReplicaSet application.

SOLUTION
Create a NodePort Service for the Existing ReplicaSet
Step 1: Inspect the Existing ReplicaSet & Pods
First, confirm the ReplicaSet's pod selector and labels so the Service targets the correct pods:

bash
kubectl get rs nginx-replicaset -o wide
kubectl get pods -l app=nginx_app,type=front-end --show-labels
Verify the pod template labels match app=nginx_app and type=front-end:

bash
kubectl get rs nginx-replicaset -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.metadata.labels}{"\n"}'
kubectl get rs nginx-replicaset -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector.matchLabels}{"\n"}'
⚠️ The Service selector must match the pod labels (not the ReplicaSet labels). Use the output above to confirm. The instructions state the pods carry app=nginx_app and type=front-end, so we'll use those.

Step 2: Create the NodePort Service
You can create it declaratively (recommended) or imperatively.

Option A: Declarative (recommended)
Create a file nginx-service.yaml:

yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: nginx-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: nginx_app
    type: front-end
  ports:
    - name: http
      protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30080
Apply it:

bash
kubectl apply -f nginx-service.yaml
Option B: Imperative (quick)
bash
kubectl expose rs nginx-replicaset \
  --name=nginx-service \
  --type=NodePort \
  --port=80 \
  --target-port=80 \
  --selector=app=nginx_app,type=front-end

# Then set the specific NodePort
kubectl patch service nginx-service -p '{"spec":{"ports":[{"name":"http","protocol":"TCP","port":80,"targetPort":80,"nodePort":30080}]}}'
Note: kubectl expose assigns a random NodePort, so the patch step is required to force 30080.

Step 3: Verify the Service
bash
# Check service details
kubectl get svc nginx-service -o wide

# Confirm NodePort, selector, and endpoints
kubectl describe svc nginx-service
kubectl get endpoints nginx-service
Expected results:

TYPE = NodePort

PORT(S) = 80:30080/TCP

Endpoints should list the IPs of the pods managed by nginx-replicaset (e.g., 10.x.x.x:80,...). If endpoints are empty, the selector doesn't match the pod labels — recheck Step 1.

Step 4: Test Access (optional)
Get a node IP and curl the NodePort:

bash
NODE_IP=$(kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.addresses[?(@.type=="InternalIP")].address}')
echo "Node IP: $NODE_IP"

# From a node, or a host that can reach the node:
curl -s http://$NODE_IP:30080 | head
Or test via a temporary pod inside the cluster:

bash
kubectl run curl-test --rm -it --image=curlimages/curl --restart=Never -- \
  curl -s http://nginx-service.default.svc.cluster.local:80 | head
Full Command Sequence
bash
# 1. Confirm pod labels
kubectl get pods -l app=nginx_app,type=front-end --show-labels

# 2. Create the service (declarative)
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: nginx-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: nginx_app
    type: front-end
  ports:
    - name: http
      protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30080
EOF

# 3. Verify
kubectl get svc nginx-service -o wide
kubectl get endpoints nginx-service
Notes
No changes to the ReplicaSet: This only adds a Service object; the ReplicaSet, pods, and their configuration remain untouched.

NodePort range: 30080 is within the default valid range (30000–32767), so no API server flag changes are needed.

Selector matching: The Service selector uses equality-based matching on the pod labels. Both app: nginx_app and type: front-end must match; if pods have additional labels, that's fine — only the listed keys need to match.

Port vs targetPort: port: 80 is the Service port (cluster-internal), targetPort: 80 is the container port nginx listens on, and nodePort: 30080 is the port exposed on every node.

